%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level4_DigitalForensicsLab
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_4
  levelNumber: 4
  displayName: Digital Forensics Lab
  sceneName: Room4_DigitalForensicsLab
  description: Trace the evidence. Expose the scam. Compare emails, web pages and
    records before you decide.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 868639aac13a5e34aab9a889c77ee51d, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 4: DIGITAL FORENSICS LAB'
  briefingText: Several suspicious communications have been reported to the cybersecurity
    team. Examine the available evidence, identify fraudulent messages, and submit
    your findings. Some communications are legitimate. Investigate before making
    your decision.
  caseNotes:
  - You store files with NovaCloud. Your plan is paid until next month.
  - You ordered headphones from Brightway Store (order BW-55120).
  - You work with Vertexa Systems as a contractor.
  - You do not have a BrightPay account.
  objectives:
  - Investigate the available case files.
  - Classify the seven reported communications as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
  - 'Inspect the evidence wall: initial email, domain record, follow-up message.'
  - Submit your findings at the final case review terminal.
  messages:
  - id: l4_account_verification
    channel: 1
    sender: NovaCloud Account Team
    senderDetail: account-team@novacloud-verify.example
    subject: 'Action needed: verify your account within 24 hours'
    body: We could not confirm your account details. To avoid restrictions on your
      storage, verify your information within 24 hours using the secure link below.
    link: novacloud-verify.example/confirm
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The display name says NovaCloud, but the address uses novacloud-verify.example,
      which is not NovaCloud's verified domain. It adds a 24-hour deadline and asks
      you to verify through its own link instead of the service you already use.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Verify your account information through the provided link within
      24 hours.
    linkNote: novacloud-verify.example/confirm (training placeholder, never opened).
    clues:
    - Claims to be NovaCloud.
    - 'Deadline: 24 hours, or storage is restricted.'
    - Compare the sender domain with Verified Domains on the network station.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_security_notification
    channel: 1
    sender: NovaCloud Security
    senderDetail: security@novacloud.example
    subject: Sign-in attempt blocked
    body: We blocked a sign-in attempt to your NovaCloud account at 09:12 from an
      unrecognised device. No action is needed if this wasn't you - your account
      is protected. To review your activity, open the NovaCloud app or go to novacloud.example
      yourself. We will never ask for your password or payment by email.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: Fictional training example. The sender domain and the event
      match the account records on the network station.
    explanation: The sender uses NovaCloud's verified domain and the 09:12 blocked
      attempt matches your account record. It asks for nothing and tells you to open
      the service yourself. This is a fictional example, not a promise that any email
      in this format is safe - still check important alerts in the official app.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Nothing. Optionally review activity in the app or at the address
      you already know.
    linkNote: No link. It points you to the app or the known address.
    clues:
    - 'Describes a specific event: blocked sign-in at 09:12.'
    - Asks for no password, code or payment.
    - Compare with Account & Order Records on the network station.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_payment_recovery
    channel: 0
    sender: BrightPay Support
    senderDetail: +1 (555) 014-3392
    subject: 
    body: 'BrightPay: your account is frozen with $480 waiting. To release the funds,
      pay a $25 recovery processing fee today at brightpay-release.example. Case
      ref: unavailable.'
    link: brightpay-release.example
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: You don't have a BrightPay account, the message comes from an ordinary
      number, it gives no case reference you can check, and it asks for a fee to
      release money. Unexpected fees to recover funds or access are a classic scam.
    trigger: 1
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Pay a $25 processing fee to release frozen funds.
    linkNote: brightpay-release.example (training placeholder, never opened).
    clues:
    - Sent from an ordinary phone number.
    - No verifiable case reference.
    - Asks for a fee to get money back.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_impersonated_announcement
    channel: 1
    sender: Vertexa Systems
    senderDetail: news@vertexa-systems-update.example
    subject: 'Required: install the new device security tool'
    body: 'All Vertexa staff and contractors must install the new device security
      tool by Friday. Download it from the update portal below and sign in with your
      work password. Questions: call +1 (555) 019-2274.'
    link: vertexa-systems-update.example/portal
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The display name is correct, but the real address is vertexa-systems-update.example,
      not Vertexa's documented domain vertexa.example. The phone number doesn't match
      the company profile either. Always read the full address and verify through
      the company's known contacts.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Download a tool from a portal and sign in with your work password.
    linkNote: vertexa-systems-update.example/portal (training placeholder, never
      opened).
    clues:
    - 'Display name: Vertexa Systems.'
    - Asks for your work password on a download portal.
    - Compare the domain and phone number with Verified Domains.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_order_update
    channel: 0
    sender: Brightway Store
    senderDetail: Saved contact
    subject: 
    body: 'Brightway: your order BW-55120 (wireless headphones) has shipped and should
      arrive Thursday. Track it in the Brightway app or your account at brightway.example.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: You ordered headphones from Brightway Store. The reference matches
      the order record on the network station.
    explanation: The order number, product and store all match your purchase record,
      it comes from the saved contact, and it only points you to the app or the store's
      known address. Context matters - but an unexpected request for payment or a
      password would still need checking.
    trigger: 1
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Nothing; optionally track the order in the app you already use.
    linkNote: No link to tap - it names the store's known address.
    clues:
    - Order BW-55120, wireless headphones.
    - From a saved contact.
    - Compare with Account & Order Records.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_prize_claim
    channel: 1
    sender: Lumen Rewards
    senderDetail: winners@lumen-rewards-prize.example
    subject: Congratulations - you've won a tablet!
    body: You were selected as this month's winner of a new tablet! To release your
      prize, pay the $4.99 delivery fee and confirm your date of birth and card details
      on the claim page within 48 hours.
    link: lumen-rewards-prize.example/claim
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: You never entered a Lumen Rewards draw, the prize needs an upfront
      fee, and it asks for your date of birth and card details. Unexpected prizes
      that need payment or sensitive information are warning signs.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Pay a delivery fee and give date of birth and card details within
      48 hours.
    linkNote: lumen-rewards-prize.example/claim (training placeholder, never opened).
    clues:
    - Prize from a competition you don't recall entering.
    - Upfront fee plus sensitive personal details.
    - 'Deadline: 48 hours.'
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_suspicious_website
    channel: 2
    sender: NovaCloud - Sign in
    senderDetail: novacl0ud-login.example
    subject: 'Captured page: NovaCloud sign-in'
    body: 'NOVACLOUD

      Sign in to keep your files safe.

      Email address 
      [__________]

      Password  [__________]

      Card number (identity check) 
      [__________]

      [ SIGN IN ]


      Footer: (c) 2026 Nova Cloud Services
      Ltd  -  Help: help@novacloud-assist.example  -  (padlock icon shown)'
    link: https://novacl0ud-login.example/signin
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The page copies NovaCloud's look, but the domain novacl0ud-login.example
      is not NovaCloud's, the company name in the footer differs, the help address
      uses yet another domain, and it asks for a card number to sign in. A padlock
      or https only means the connection is encrypted - not that the site is who
      it claims to be.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: Enter your email, password and card number to sign in.
    linkNote: novacl0ud-login.example (training placeholder, never opened). The padlock
      only means the connection is encrypted.
    clues:
    - 'Address: novacl0ud-login.example.'
    - 'Footer company: Nova Cloud Services Ltd; help: novacloud-assist.example.'
    - Asks for a card number to sign in.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_final_case
    channel: 3
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: "CASE-04 \xB7 Billing Storm"
    subject: "CASE-04  \xB7  Operation Billing Storm"
    body: 'A wave of "payment failed" messages has reached staff who use NovaCloud.
      Using the initial email, the domain record and the follow-up message, decide:
      is this campaign a scam or legitimate billing contact from NovaCloud?'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'Several independent indicators agree: the email comes from novacloud-support.example,
      which is not NovaCloud''s verified domain; that domain was registered 3 days
      ago with a hidden owner and shares a server with the fake sign-in page; the
      follow-up text from an unknown number repeats the same link and pressure; and
      your account record shows the plan is paid. No single clue decides it - together
      they do.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues:
    - 'Email sender: billing@novacloud-support.example - NovaCloud''s verified domain
      is novacloud.example.'
    - Domain registered 3 days ago, owner hidden, same server as novacl0ud-login.example.
    - Follow-up text from an unknown number with the same link and a 'final notice'.
    - Your NovaCloud plan is paid until next month.
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l4_old_welcome
    channel: 1
    sender: Forensics Team Lead
    senderDetail: lead@cyberlab.example
    subject: Your first case in the lab
    body: Welcome to the forensics lab. Reported messages arrive here and on your
      phone. The evidence wall and the network analysis station hold the records
      you need - compare before you classify. The final case review terminal unlocks
      once the evidence is in.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  - id: l4_old_receipt
    channel: 1
    sender: Brightway Store
    senderDetail: orders@brightway.example
    subject: Receipt for order BW-55120
    body: 'Thanks for your order: wireless headphones, paid by card. Track it in
      the Brightway app or at brightway.example.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
  caseFiles:
  - title: Initial Email
    body: '<b>From:</b> NovaCloud Billing  <billing@novacloud-support.example>

      <b>Received:</b>
      today 08:41

      <b>Subject:</b> Payment failed - update your card within
      12 hours


      "Your storage plan payment failed. Update your card within
      12 hours at novacloud-support.example/billing or your files will be deleted."


      <b>Asks
      for:</b> card details on a linked page

      <b>Link:</b> novacloud-support.example/billing
      (training placeholder)'
  - title: Domain Record
    body: '<b>Domain:</b> novacloud-support.example

      <b>Registered:</b> 3 days
      ago

      <b>Owner:</b> hidden (privacy service)

      <b>Server:</b> 203.0.113.48
      - also hosts novacl0ud-login.example (the fake sign-in page)

      <b>Listed
      by NovaCloud:</b> no - NovaCloud lists only novacloud.example


      This
      is a fictional training record; it does not describe any real domain.'
  - title: Follow-up Message
    body: '<b>Text message from:</b> +1 (555) 017-8820 (not in contacts)

      <b>Received:</b>
      today 10:05


      "NovaCloud FINAL NOTICE: billing failed. Update at novacloud-support.example/billing
      or lose all files today."


      <b>Same link as the email:</b> yes

      <b>Pressure:</b>
      ''final notice'', ''lose all files today'''
  - title: Case Notes
    body: "\u2022  Reports came from 14 staff between 08:40 and 10:10.\n\u2022  All
      messages point to the same domain.\n\u2022  NovaCloud's billing page is inside
      its app; it doesn't delete files over one failed payment.\n\u2022  Compare
      the records on the network analysis station before submitting the final case."
  firstMessageDelay: 10
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 10, y: 20}
  minimumActivityDelay: 5
  scoring:
    basePoints: 250
    speedBonus: 25
    fastWindow: 0.35
    wrongPenalty: 50
    recoveryPoints: 60
    baseReadingSeconds: 20
    secondsPerWord: 0.5
    secondsPerDetail: 3
    minReadingSeconds: 40
    maxReadingSeconds: 120
    caseExtraSeconds: 75
    overtimeStepSeconds: 10
    overtimePenaltyPerStep: 10
    maxTimePenalty: 60
    minCorrectPoints: 190
  oneStarScore: 1
  twoStarScore: 1500
  threeStarScore: 1700
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 1500
  nextLevelId: level_5
  nextSceneName: Room5_MacLab
  lessons:
  - A familiar display name proves nothing - read the full sender address and compare
    it with verified records.
  - 'Security alerts can be genuine: check whether the details match your records
    and whether it asks for anything.'
  - Fees to recover money, release funds or claim a prize are major warning signs.
  - A professional page or a padlock doesn't prove a site is real - check the domain
    and verify the organisation.
  - Strong conclusions come from several independent clues pointing the same way.
  learningSummary: You compared communications against verified domains and account
    records, exposed impersonation, fee scams and a fake sign-in page, recognised
    genuine notices, and tied several pieces of evidence together to close a campaign.
  performanceHigh: Outstanding forensic work! Every conclusion was backed by the
    evidence.
  performanceModerate: Good investigation. Review the explanations - the records
    hold the clues that separate real from fake.
  performanceLow: Keep practising. Compare each sender, domain and request with the
    records before you decide.
